  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 149 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nFriday through Monday:\n\nFriday and Saturday will continue to be MUCH warmer than \nnormal, approaching record but not quite, with highs in the \nlower 60s. \n\nWinds will temporarily weaken Thursday night into Friday \nmorning, but the next surface low developing over the Northern \nPlains will track through northern MN and Ontario Friday night \nthrough Saturday and strengthen the southerly winds again. This \ntime, there should be an area of showers (thunderstorms not \nexpected) along the associated cold front as it swings across WI\non Saturday.\n\nThe forecast for Saturday has an approximately 6 hour period of\nrain likely (55-70%) Saturday afternoon/evening. The forecast \nprecip amount is around 0.10 inch, so quite light. This is taken\nfrom the NBM (National Blend of Models) and it seems \nreasonable. The GFS and ECMWF are very similar in their upper \nlevel and surface low patterns until Saturday morning, but then \nthe GFS tries to strengthen the low more than the ECMWF as it \ncrosses Lake Superior. The GFS has a tendency to over-do the \ncyclogenesis in low pressure systems, so taking a model blend \napproach at this time is prudent. The less-amplified solution \nwould suggest lower precip amounts, but it is also slower- \nmoving. \n\nHigh pressure will slide across the Upper Great Lakes Sunday \nnight through Tuesday and we can expect much cooler \ntemperatures. However, these temps are right around normal for \nlate November.\n\nCronce",
